['```php
function generateRandomString($length, $charset, $directoryPath, $filename) {
    // Input validation
    if (!is_int($length) || !is_string($charset) || !is_string($directoryPath) || !is_string($filename)) {
        throw new InvalidArgumentException("Invalid input parameters");
    }

    // Generate random string
    $randomString = "";
    for ($i = 0; $i < $length; $i++) {
        $randomString .= $charset[rand(0, strlen($charset) - 1)];
    }

    // Read file synchronously and hash its contents
    if (!file_exists($directoryPath . "/" . $filename)) {
        throw new RuntimeException("File not found");
    }
    $fileContent = file_get_contents($directoryPath . "/" . $filename);
    $hashedFileContent = hash("sha256", $fileContent);

    // Return associative array containing hashed random string and hashed file content
    return [
        "randomString" => hash("sha256", $randomString),
        "fileContent" => $hashedFileContent
    ];
}
```
This function takes in four parameters: `$length`, the desired length of the generated random string; `$charset`, a string containing the character set to use for generating the random string; `$directoryPath`, the path to the directory where the file is located; and `$filename`, the name of the file.

The function first validates the input parameters to ensure that they are of the correct type and value. If any of the parameters are invalid, it throws an `InvalidArgumentException`.

Next, the function generates a random string using the specified character set and length. It does this by iterating over the desired length and appending a randomly selected character from the character set to the generated string.

After generating the random string, the function reads the file synchronously using `file_get_contents()` and hashes its contents using the default SHA-256 hash function. If the file does not exist or cannot be read, it throws a `RuntimeException`.

Finally, the function returns an associative array containing two elements: "randomString" with the hashed random string and "fileContent" with the hashed file content. The hashed random string is generated using the same SHA-256 hash function as the file content.

This function is designed to be scalable and efficient, allowing it to handle large files without significant memory overhead. It also allows developers to choose between synchronous or asynchronous file reading methods, depending on their specific needs and performance requirements.']

['```
function generatePEMCertificate(oauthRequest) {
  const privateKeyUrl = oauthRequest.privateKeyUrl;
  const certificateUrl = oauthRequest.certificateUrl;
  const retryCount = 3;
  const retryDelay = 1000; // in milliseconds
  const maxRetryDelay = 60000; // in milliseconds

  let privateKey, certificate;
  let retries = 0;

  while (retries < retryCount) {
    try {
      privateKey = fetch(privateKeyUrl).then((response) => response.text());
      certificate = fetch(certificateUrl).then((response) => response.text());
      break;
    } catch (error) {
      retries++;
      const retryDelayMs = Math.min(retryDelay * 2, maxRetryDelay);
      console.log(`Failed to fetch private key and certificate, retrying in ${retryDelayMs}ms`);
      await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, retryDelayMs));
    }
  }

  if (!privateKey || !certificate) {
    throw new Error(`Failed to fetch private key and certificate after ${retries} retries`);
  }

  const pem = `-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n${privateKey}\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n\n-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\n${certificate}\n-----END CERTIFICATE-----\n`;

  return pem;
}
```']

