using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class DynamicRangeManager
{
    private readonly List<(int Min, int Max)> _ranges = new();

    public void AddRange(int min, int max)
    {
        if (min > max) throw new ArgumentException("Min must be less than or equal to Max");
        _ranges.Add((min, max));
    }

    public void RemoveRange(int min, int max)
    {
        var range = _ranges.FirstOrDefault(x => x.Min == min && x.Max == max);
        if (range != default)
            _ranges.Remove(range);
    }

    public void MergeRanges(int min1, int max1, int min2, int max2)
    {
        var range1 = _ranges.FirstOrDefault(x => x.Min == min1 && x.Max == max1);
        var range2 = _ranges.FirstOrDefault(x => x.Min == min2 && x.Max == max2);
        if (range1 != default && range2 != default)
        {
            var newRange = (Math.Min(range1.Min, range2.Min), Math.Max(range1.Max, range2.Max));
            _ranges.Remove(range1);
            _ranges.Remove(range2);
            _ranges.Add(newRange);
        }
    }

    public void SplitRange(int min, int max, Func<int, bool> condition)
    {
        var range = _ranges.FirstOrDefault(x => x.Min == min && x.Max == max);
        if (range != default)
        {
            var splitIndex = _ranges.FindIndex(x => x.Min == min && x.Max == max);
            var newRange1 = (range.Min, splitIndex);
            var newRange2 = (splitIndex + 1, range.Max);
            _ranges[splitIndex] = newRange1;
            _ranges.Insert(splitIndex + 1, newRange2);
        }
    }

    public int GenerateRandomNumber()
    {
        var random = new Random();
        while (true)
        {
            var number = random.Next(int.MinValue, int.MaxValue);
            if (_ranges.Any(x => x.Min <= number && x.Max >= number))
                return number;
        }
    }

    public override string ToString()
    {
        var ranges = _ranges.Select(x => $"{x.Min} - {x.Max}");
        return string.Join(", ", ranges);
    }
}
```
This code defines a `DynamicRangeManager` class that can efficiently handle a dynamic collection of numerical ranges, each defined by a minimum and maximum value, and can be either inclusive or exclusive. The class provides methods to add, remove, merge, and split ranges, as well as generate random numbers within the specified ranges.

The `AddRange` method adds a new range to the list of ranges, while the `RemoveRange` method removes an existing range from the list. The `MergeRanges` method merges two overlapping ranges into a single range with the widest possible bounds, and the `SplitRange` method splits a range into two non-overlapping ranges based on a specified condition.

The `GenerateRandomNumber` method generates a random number within the specified ranges by repeatedly generating random numbers until one falls within an existing range. The `ToString` method provides a clear and concise representation of all active ranges for debugging purposes.

To optimize performance, the class uses a list of tuples to store the ranges, which allows for efficient insertion, removal, and iteration operations. Additionally, the `GenerateRandomNumber` method uses a random number generator to generate random numbers within the specified ranges, which ensures that no redundant objects are created.

Overall, this code provides an efficient and flexible way to handle dynamic numerical ranges in C#.']

