The program dynamically allocates memory for each line when processing a large dataset spread across multiple text files containing measurements. It computes the average of the last three valid numeric measurements for each set and appends this average as the next value in the sequence. Missing or malformed data is handled gracefully, ensuring stability even if some lines contain non-numeric values.

The program integrates with an external data validation service to assess measurement quality, influencing whether the measurement is included in the processing pipeline. It also manages efficient memory management to avoid leaks and scalable performance with large datasets, balancing optimization with ease of maintenance.

Considering the engineering trade-offs mentioned earlier, this code optimizes for quick processing but ensures memory usage remains low, especially when dealing with a high number of small files. It also designs the program to handle a growing number of files and larger datasets without significant performance degradation. Additionally, it implements comprehensive error handling to maintain stability, especially when dealing with unexpected input or service disruptions.

The code is structured to facilitate future enhancements and debugging, balancing complexity with clarity. It also ensures seamless interaction with external services while minimizing latency and network overhead.']

