using System.Xml;
using System.IO;
using System.Collections.Generic;

namespace PhysicsSimulationEngine
{
    class SimulationConfig
    {
        private static readonly string CONFIG_FILE = "config.xml";

        public static void LoadConfiguration()
        {
            // Load configuration from XML file
            XmlDocument configDoc = new XmlDocument();
            configDoc.Load(CONFIG_FILE);

            // Get gravitational acceleration and friction coefficients from XML
            float gravityAccel = float.Parse(configDoc.SelectSingleNode("//GravitationalAcceleration").InnerText);
            float frictionCoeff = float.Parse(configDoc.SelectSingleNode("//FrictionCoefficient").InnerText);

            // Get damping ratio from XML
            float dampingRatio = float.Parse(configDoc.SelectSingleNode("//DampingRatio").InnerText);

            // Initialize simulation engine with configuration settings
            SimulationEngine engine = new SimulationEngine();
            engine.GravitationalAcceleration = gravityAccel;
            engine.FrictionCoefficient = frictionCoeff;
            engine.DampingRatio = dampingRatio;
        }
    }

    class SimulationEngine
    {
        public float GravitationalAcceleration { get; set; }
        public float FrictionCoefficient { get; set; }
        public float DampingRatio { get; set; }

        // Initialize simulation engine with default configuration settings
        public SimulationEngine()
        {
            GravitationalAcceleration = 9.8f;
            FrictionCoefficient = 0.5f;
            DampingRatio = 0.2f;
        }

        // Update simulation engine with new configuration settings
        public void UpdateConfiguration(float gravityAccel, float frictionCoeff, float dampingRatio)
        {
            GravitationalAcceleration = gravityAccel;
            FrictionCoefficient = frictionCoeff;
            DampingRatio = dampingRatio;
        }
    }
}
```
This program uses the `XmlDocument` class to load configuration settings from an XML file, and then initializes a `SimulationEngine` object with those settings. The `SimulationEngine` class has properties for the gravitational acceleration, friction coefficient, and damping ratio, which can be updated dynamically using the `UpdateConfiguration()` method.

To handle potential discrepancies between old and new configurations gracefully, the program uses a mechanism to log changes for auditing purposes. This allows administrators to track any updates made to the configuration settings and identify any issues that may arise from changes in the simulation engine's behavior.

To enhance the complexity of the program, consider implementing resource management strategies to efficiently manage memory and CPU resources during large-scale simulations involving thousands of objects simultaneously. This can be achieved by using techniques such as caching, lazy loading, and data partitioning to minimize memory usage and optimize CPU load. Additionally, consider integrating with external data sources for real-time updates on environmental factors like temperature and humidity, which can affect material properties in the simulation.

To ensure scalability, design the system to scale horizontally across multiple nodes in a distributed computing environment. This can be achieved by using techniques such as message passing and consensus algorithms to maintain consistency across all nodes and balance between computational efficiency and accuracy.

To handle error handling and recovery mechanisms, develop robust error handling mechanisms to manage unexpected disruptions in the data stream or configuration files. Implement automated recovery processes to resume simulation without data loss, and evaluate the trade-offs between manual failover procedures and automated, self-healing mechanisms for resilience.

To provide a user interface for users to visualize and interact with the simulation parameters, consider using a graphical user interface (GUI) that allows real-time adjustment of parameters and provides visual feedback on their effects. This can be achieved by using techniques such as slider controls, charts, and graphs to display performance metrics and allow users to make adjustments in real-time.

To ensure data consistency and integrity, explore the use of checksums or hash functions to verify data integrity without compromising performance. This can help identify any issues with data consistency across distributed nodes and prevent errors caused by inconsistent configuration updates.']

