```
use std::fs;
use std::path::PathBuf;
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use std::time::Instant;

struct DirectoryCreator {
    // The path to the directory that will be created
    path: PathBuf,

    // The permissions to set on the directory
    permissions: fs::Permissions,

    // A lock used to coordinate access to the directory creation process
    lock: Arc<Mutex<()>>,
}

impl DirectoryCreator {
    fn new(path: PathBuf, permissions: fs::Permissions) -> Self {
        Self {
            path,
            permissions,
            lock: Arc::new(Mutex::new(()))
        }
    }

    // Creates a new directory with the specified name and permissions
    fn create_directory(&self) -> Result<(), std::io::Error> {
        let mut lock = self.lock.lock().unwrap();

        // Check if the parent directory exists
        if !fs::metadata(self.path.parent()).is_ok() {
            return Err(std::io::Error::new(
                std::io::ErrorKind::NotFound,
                format!("Parent directory does not exist: {}", self.path.parent().display())
            ));
        }

        // Create the new directory with the specified permissions
        fs::create_dir(&self.path).map_err(|e| {
            std::io::Error::new(std::io::ErrorKind::Other, e)
        })?;

        Ok(())
    }
}
```
This implementation uses the `fs` module to interact with the file system and the `Mutex` type from the `sync` crate to coordinate access to the directory creation process. The `DirectoryCreator` struct has a `path` field that stores the path to the directory that will be created, a `permissions` field that stores the permissions to set on the directory, and an `lock` field that is used to coordinate access to the directory creation process.

The `create_directory` method first acquires a lock on the `DirectoryCreator` instance using the `Mutex::lock` method. This ensures that only one thread can create a new directory at a time, which helps prevent race conditions and improves performance.

Next, the method checks if the parent directory exists by calling the `fs::metadata` method on the parent path of the directory to be created. If the parent directory does not exist, the method returns an error with a message indicating that the parent directory does not exist.

If the parent directory exists, the method creates the new directory using the `fs::create_dir` method. This method takes the path to the directory as an argument and creates it if it does not already exist. If there is an error creating the directory, the method returns an error with a message indicating what went wrong.

Finally, the method releases the lock on the `DirectoryCreator` instance using the `Mutex::unlock` method. This ensures that other threads can access the directory creation process after it has been completed.

