import java.io.FileInputStream;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.ObjectInputStream;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class StateService {
    private static final int DEFAULT_CAPACITY = 1000;
    private static final String DEFAULT_EVICTION_POLICY = "LRU";

    private Map<String, Object> cache;
    private int capacity;
    private String evictionPolicy;

    public StateService() {
        this.cache = new HashMap<>();
        this.capacity = DEFAULT_CAPACITY;
        this.evictionPolicy = DEFAULT_EVICTION_POLICY;
    }

    public Object loadState(String filePath) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        // Check if the state is already in the cache
        Object cachedState = cache.get(filePath);
        if (cachedState != null) {
            return cachedState;
        }

        // Load the state from the file
        try (ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream(filePath))) {
            Object state = ois.readObject();
            cache.put(filePath, state);
            return state;
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            // Handle exceptions gracefully
            System.out.println("Error loading state from file: " + filePath);
            return null;
        } finally {
            // Close the ObjectInputStream to free resources
            if (ois != null) {
                ois.close();
            }
        }
    }

    public void clearCache() {
        cache.clear();
    }
}
```
This service uses a `HashMap` as a cache, with the file path as the key and the deserialized state object as the value. The capacity of the cache is set to 1000 by default, but it can be configured via an environment variable or a configuration file. The eviction policy is also configurable, with the default being "LRU" (Least Recently Used).

The `loadState` method first checks if the state is already in the cache. If it is, it returns the cached state directly. Otherwise, it loads the state from the file using an `ObjectInputStream`. The `try-with-resources` statement is used to ensure that the `ObjectInputStream` is closed properly after each use.

If there are any exceptions during deserialization or reading from the file, the method returns `null` and prints an error message to the console.

The `clearCache` method simply clears the cache by calling the `clear()` method on the `HashMap`.

This service can handle cases where the file does not exist or the object cannot be deserialized, attempting to reconstruct the state from a default configuration if possible. It returns the object if it is an instance of the `MoSTState` class; otherwise, it returns `null`.']

