```java
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class StringHasher {
    private static final ConcurrentHashMap<String, byte[]> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public static byte[] md5(String input) {
        // Check if the input is already in the cache
        byte[] hash = cache.get(input);
        if (hash != null) {
            return hash;
        }

        // If not, compute the MD5 hash and add it to the cache
        try {
            MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
            byte[] bytes = input.getBytes();
            md.update(bytes);
            hash = md.digest();
            cache.put(input, hash);
            return hash;
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            // Handle the error by printing an error message and returning null
            System.err.println("Error: failed to compute MD5 hash");
            return null;
        }
    }
}
```
This method takes a `String` input as its only parameter, and returns its MD5 hash as a `byte[]`. It uses a `ConcurrentHashMap` to cache previously computed hashes for frequently requested strings. If the input is already in the cache, it returns the cached value. Otherwise, it computes the MD5 hash using `MessageDigest` and adds it to the cache before returning it. If an error occurs while computing the hash, it handles the error by printing an error message and returning null.']

